  "narrative": "During the 1930s, Black and African women's hairstyles were deeply rooted in cultural identity and tradition. While there is limited documentation on hair culture during this specific era, it is clear that afros, cornrows, and other braided styles were prominent among African cultures and their diasporic communities. These hairstyles not only served as a means of self-expression but also as a way to preserve and celebrate one's heritage. In the absence of more detailed information on hair culture during this time, it is likely that protective hairstyles such as Bantu knots and box braids were also practiced, as they are considered traditional styles in many African cultures. The significance of these hairstyles lies not only in their aesthetic appeal but also in their role as a symbol of identity, community, and resistance against the erasure of Black beauty standards.",
